#x02021;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x0002b;2.3</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;1.6<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-176">176</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R2Q</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x0002b;3.6<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-176">176</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;0.5</td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R3Q<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-152">&#x02021;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x0002b;8.6<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-176">176</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;1.6<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-176">176</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R4Q</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x0002b;0.4</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x0002b;0.9</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="TFIV-150"><label>&#x0002a;</label><p>v<sub>1/2</sub> and z<sub>m</sub> are the half-maximal voltage and gating valence of activation.  Data from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B41">St&#x000fc;hmer et al. (1989)</xref> did not include an assessment of statistical significance. &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-152"><label>&#x02021;</label><p>Data from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B6">Chen et al. (1996)</xref>. &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-153"><label>&#x000a7;</label><p>Data from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B41">St&#x000fc;hmer et al.  (1989)</xref>. &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-155"><label>&#x02016;</label><p>The values for z<sub>m</sub> from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B41">St&#x000fc;hmer et al. (1989)</xref> were multiplied by  three for comparison because those values were based on three identical  gating charges (m<sup>3</sup>). &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-176"><label>176</label><p>Values statistically significant, with a probability &#x0003c;0.05 resulting from random variation.    &#x000a0;</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><ack><p>We thank Dr. Raymond Smith, Dr. Michael Pugsley, Ted Shih, Marianne Smith, and Dan Allen f